course: dictionary
lesson: 8822
title: Shepherd: a person, a verb, a dog
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which meaning the writer means. This is context. «The shepherd counted his sheep before night fell.» Sheep are the clue here, so a shepherd must be a person who looks after sheep.
explain: Shepherd can also be a verb, a doing word. «The teacher shepherded the children onto the bus.» Children and a bus are the clues. To shepherd people means to guide them carefully somewhere. Check by swapping in your meaning: "The teacher guided the children carefully onto the bus" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Look at the words before the word too. In «German shepherds», the word German tells you this is a name for a kind of dog. «Police forces use dogs, very often German shepherds, to track people.» The dogs are tracking and searching, so the clue points to a dog, not a person.
fact: A shepherd is a person whose job is to look after sheep, keeping them safe and moving them to fresh grass.
fact: As a verb, «shepherd» means to guide people carefully somewhere, like a teacher shepherding children onto a bus.
fact: A German shepherd is a large kind of dog, strong and quick to learn.
fact: In «The Boy Who Cried Wolf», a shepherd boy lies for fun, and when a real wolf comes, nobody believes him.
fact: Sometimes the same word has different meanings, so always check the clues in the sentence.
exercise: «The shepherd counted the sheep before night fell.» What is a shepherd here? | A farm gate | *A person who looks after sheep | A teacher on a school trip | A large kind of dog
exercise: «Mrs Patel shepherded the class across the busy road.» What does shepherded mean? | Ate lunch quickly | Counted the children | *Guided carefully | Pushed hard and ran
exercise: «Police forces use dogs, very often German shepherds, to track people.» What kind of animal is a German shepherd here? | A farm building | *A large kind of dog | A sheep | A horse with a long tail
test: «In Aesop's fable, a shepherd boy cried "Wolf!" for fun so many times that nobody believed him.» What lesson does this story teach? | *Trust is built on honesty, and once it is broken it is hard to rebuild | Wolves are always near farms | Shepherds are never believed | Boys should never go outdoors
test: «Angels sent shepherds to see the baby lying in a manger.» In this sentence, what are shepherds? | *People who look after sheep | Angels with wings | Dogs trained to find babies | Travellers from a far country
exam: In «The teacher shepherded the children onto the bus», what does shepherded mean? | A large kind of dog | A person who looks after sheep | *To guide carefully | To count sheep at night
